'use strict';
// A synthetic, varied long spoken answer to "Describe your town" (no real student; the town is invented). Good quality, no repeated
// sentences, fillers already removed (as the transcription worker stores the assessed text). Used by the speaking benchmark
// (tests/fixtures/audio/speakingBenchmark.cjs) and so by scripts/speakingAssessmentTrial.js.
const PARAGRAPHS = [
  'I live in Riverton, a medium-sized town in the north of the country, about two hours by train from the capital. Most people who visit for the first time notice the river before anything else, because it curves right through the centre and splits the town into an old half and a new half. I have lived here my whole life, so when I describe it, I am really describing the place that made me who I am.',
  'The old half grew up around the market square. The buildings there are narrow and tall, with painted shutters and small balconies full of plants. On the ground floor you find bakeries, a shoe repair shop, a pharmacy and a tea house that has been open since my grandfather was a boy. The streets are made of stone, which looks beautiful in photographs but is not very comfortable if you are pulling a heavy suitcase.',
  'The new half is completely different. After the bridge was widened, the council allowed builders to put up blocks of flats, a shopping centre and a large sports hall. Many young families moved there because the flats are cheaper and the rooms are bigger. My aunt lives on the seventh floor of one of those buildings, and from her window you can see the whole valley, including the hills where we go walking in spring.',
  'Daily life in Riverton starts early. By six o\'clock the delivery vans are already queuing outside the market, and the fishermen are carrying boxes of fish up from the river. My mother says that if you want the best vegetables you have to arrive before seven, because after that the restaurant owners have taken everything good. I usually go with her on Saturdays, and I enjoy listening to people bargaining, joking and sharing news.',
  'During the week the town feels busy but not stressful. Children walk or cycle to school, office workers wait for the bus at the bridge, and older people sit on benches near the fountain reading newspapers. There is a rhythm to it that I find comforting. Even when it rains, which happens a lot in autumn, people still stop to greet each other, and the shopkeepers leave umbrellas by the door for customers who forgot theirs.',
  'What I value most about my town is the sense of community. When a family has a problem, neighbours usually notice and help without being asked. Two winters ago, our street flooded after a storm, and the water came into several houses. Within an hour, people had brought sandbags, buckets and dry clothes. Nobody organised it officially; it simply happened because people care about each other. That memory makes me proud to live here.',
  'Riverton also has a lively cultural side, which surprises visitors who expect a quiet country town. Every summer there is a music festival on the riverbank, with local bands in the afternoon and better-known groups at night. The library runs a reading club for teenagers, and the community theatre puts on two plays a year. Last year my class helped paint the scenery for a comedy, and I discovered that I really enjoy working behind the stage.',
  'Food is another reason people love the town. Because of the river, fish is cheap and fresh, and almost every family has its own recipe for fish soup. My favourite place to eat is a small cafe near the station that serves rice, lentils and spicy vegetables at prices that students can afford. On cold evenings the owner gives free tea to anyone waiting for the last train, which tells you a lot about the kind of place this is.',
  'Of course, Riverton is not perfect, and I think it is important to be honest about its problems. The biggest one is traffic. The bridge is the only way to cross the river by car, so in the morning and the evening it becomes completely blocked. Buses get stuck in the same queues as everyone else, which means that people who cannot afford a car lose a lot of time every day just getting to work or school.',
  'Another problem is that many young people leave after finishing school. There are not enough jobs here for graduates, especially in areas like engineering, design or medicine. My cousin moved to the capital last year because she could not find work as an architect. She still visits often, but she says she cannot imagine coming back unless the situation changes. When talented people leave, the town slowly loses energy and new ideas.',
  'The environment is also a concern. In the past the river was polluted by a paper factory upstream. The factory closed ten years ago, and the water is much cleaner now, but plastic rubbish still collects along the banks after festivals and weekends. Some students at my school have started a monthly clean-up, and I join them when I can. It is hard work, but it is satisfying to see the riverbank looking tidy again.',
  'If I could change one thing about Riverton, I would build a second bridge for buses, bicycles and pedestrians only. That would reduce the traffic on the main bridge, make public transport faster and encourage more people to leave their cars at home. It would also connect the two halves of the town more closely. At the moment, people from the new half rarely visit the old square, and I think both sides miss out because of that.',
  'I would also like the council to support small businesses, especially ones started by young people. For example, empty shops in the old half could be rented cheaply to people who want to open studios, repair workshops or small technology companies. That might persuade some graduates to stay, or even attract people from other towns. A place feels alive when it offers opportunities, not only memories.',
  'My school is on the hill above the old half, and the walk there takes about twenty minutes. The building is quite old, but the teachers have made it a friendly place to learn. There is a science lab that was renovated recently, a small garden where we grow tomatoes and herbs, and a hall where the whole school meets on Monday mornings. I like it most at the end of the day, when the corridors go quiet and the evening light reaches the classrooms.',
  'Thinking about the future, I am optimistic. Riverton has a strong community, a beautiful setting and a history that people respect. If the town solves its transport problems and gives young people reasons to stay, I believe it can grow without losing its character. I do not know yet whether I will live here as an adult, because I may need to study somewhere else, but I am sure I will always come back to visit.',
  'In conclusion, my town is a mixture of old and new, busy and calm, proud and self-critical. It is the place where I learned to ride a bicycle, where I made my closest friends and where I first realised that a community is only as strong as the way people treat each other. When someone asks me where I am from, I answer with a smile, because I know exactly what Riverton means to me.'
];

const countWords = text => String(text).split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;

/** Whole paragraphs, in order, until the answer has at least `words` words (all of them when `words` is larger than the text). */
function variedAnswer(words) {
  const out = [];
  for (const paragraph of PARAGRAPHS) {
    if (out.length && countWords(out.join(' ')) >= words) break;
    out.push(paragraph);
  }
  return out.join(' ');
}

module.exports = { PARAGRAPHS, variedAnswer, countWords };
